<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Apple privacy manifest. Required since 2024 for App Store submission, and
  this is the file whose absence is rejection ITMS-91053 ("Missing API
  declaration"). No Xcode template ever ran over this project, so nothing
  created it: it is written by hand, from the code, and gated by
  `squallar/src/apple_bundle_metadata.rs`.

  ONE file, both bundles. The iOS and macOS bundles wrap the same Rust
  staticlib/binary, so the required-reason answer cannot legitimately differ
  between them; two copies could only drift. It is copied verbatim (`cp`, no
  `sed` — there are no build settings to expand here) to the two places Apple
  looks, which are NOT the same place:

      iOS    squallar.app/PrivacyInfo.xcprivacy                 (bundle root)
      macOS  squallar.app/Contents/Resources/PrivacyInfo.xcprivacy

  See packaging/ios/Makefile and packaging/macos/Makefile.

  ── Why each entry below is here ─────────────────────────────────────────

  These are Apple's "required reason APIs": ordinary system calls that an app
  must declare a reason for using. A Rust app trips them the way a .NET app
  does — through the standard library, not through anything the author wrote —
  which is exactly why nobody notices until the upload is rejected.

  NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategorySystemBootTime / 35F9.1
      The symbol that reaches this is `mach_absolute_time`, and it comes from
      **wgpu-hal's Metal backend**: `wgpu-hal/src/metal/time.rs` declares
      `extern "C" { fn mach_absolute_time() -> u64; }` and calls it in
      `PresentationTimer::get_timestamp_ns`, which is the timer Metal's
      presentation callbacks use. Both Apple targets render through Metal.
      35F9.1 is the reason for measuring elapsed time between in-app events,
      which is what a presentation timer does.

      **The obvious answer is subtly the wrong one, so it is written down.**
      `std::time::Instant` looks like the reacher — there are 238
      `Instant::now()` call sites in this workspace — but on Apple std calls
      `clock_gettime(CLOCK_UPTIME_RAW)`, not `mach_absolute_time`
      (library/std/src/sys/time/unix.rs, the `#[cfg(target_vendor = "apple")]`
      arm of `Instant::CLOCK_ID`). Apple's boot-time list names two symbols,
      `systemUptime` and `mach_absolute_time()`, and `clock_gettime` is
      neither. Std's comment that the value "is identical to the result of
      mach_absolute_time()" is a statement about the number, not about the
      symbol a binary scanner sees. So `Instant` alone would probably not trip
      ITMS-91053; wgpu-hal certainly does. The declaration is right either way,
      and it is right for a reason that survives a change to either one.

  NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryFileTimestamp / C617.1
      Four first-party reachers, all in `squallar-egui`, all inside
      `#[cfg(not(target_arch = "wasm32"))]` code, so all live on both Apple
      targets:

        basemap_archive/block_cache.rs  `mtime_nanos` — `Metadata::modified()`
                                        on the app's cached archive blocks, to
                                        pick an eviction order
        basemap_archive/block_cache.rs  `recover_totals` — `DirEntry::metadata()`
                                        to size and age the same cache at open
        basemap_download.rs             `DirEntry::metadata()` sizing the
                                        offline segments the app itself wrote
        basemap_archive.rs              `File::metadata()` for the length of the
                                        app's own archive file

      C617.1 is the reason for metadata of files inside the app's own
      container, which is what every one of those is: files this app wrote,
      under its own cache directory, read back by this app alone.

      One caveat, recorded rather than smoothed over: `chrono`'s Unix
      `Local::now()` path calls `fs::symlink_metadata("/etc/localtime")` and
      then `.modified()` on it, and the app calls `chrono::Local::now()` in
      three places. `/etc/localtime` is a *system* file, outside the app
      container, so C617.1's wording does not literally cover it — and no
      other code on Apple's list does either (DDA9.1 is display-to-user,
      3B52.1 is document-picker files, 0A2A.1 is for an SDK author's own
      manifest). The category is declared and the app has a genuine C617.1 use,
      so the manifest is truthful as it stands; this is here so the chrono
      reacher is not later found and mistaken for an undeclared one.

  NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryUserDefaults / CA92.1
      **macOS only, and reached through a dependency rather than through any
      line in this workspace** — which is exactly why it is easy to miss and
      why `apple_bundle_metadata.rs` couples it to the dependency instead of to
      a source marker.

      `dark-light` 2.0.0 (`src/platforms/macos.rs`, `#[cfg(target_os =
      "macos")]`) reads the system theme with
      `NSUserDefaults::standardUserDefaults()`, `persistentDomainForName("Apple
      Global Domain")`, `objectForKey("AppleInterfaceStyle")`. Those selectors
      land in the macOS binary's Objective-C metadata, which is what Apple's
      scanner reads. `PlatformBridge::detect_dark_theme`
      (`squallar/src/platform.rs`) calls it at startup, up to three times,
      before the first frame.

      iOS does **not** link it: the dependency is declared under
      `[target.'cfg(not(any(target_os = "android", target_os = "ios")))'.dependencies]`
      in `squallar/Cargo.toml`, and a `cargo tree` inverse query for
      `dark-light` against `aarch64-apple-ios` prints nothing. (Spelled without
      the flag on purpose: a double hyphen is illegal inside an XML comment and
      makes this whole manifest unparseable.) The declaration is therefore an
      over-declaration on iOS, which Apple does not reject; the alternative is
      two manifests that can drift, and under-declaring is the direction that
      gets a build refused. If that trade stops being worth it, the way out is
      to retire `dark-light` on macOS in favour of winit's `ThemeChanged` /
      `NSApplication.effectiveAppearance`, which reach no required-reason API
      at all — and the gate will then tell you to drop this entry.

      **CA92.1 is the best available code, not an exact one.** It reads
      "information that is accessible only to the app itself", and
      `AppleInterfaceStyle` in the global domain is a system preference rather
      than this app's own data. Nothing on Apple's list fits better: 1C8F.1 is
      app groups, AC6B.1 is managed-app configuration, C56D.1 is for an SDK
      author declaring in the SDK's own manifest. The read is local, is used
      only to pick a theme, and nothing derived from it leaves the device.
      Whether a reviewer agrees is not knowable without submitting.

  ── Categories deliberately NOT declared, each checked ────────────────────

  Declaring a category the app does not touch is not free: it is a claim in a
  submission, and the next person to read this file would believe it. Both
  remaining categories were checked — across the workspace, `vendor/`, and
  every crate in `cargo tree` for both Apple targets — and are absent.

  NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryDiskSpace
      Nothing calls `statfs`, `statvfs`, `f_bavail`/`f_bfree`,
      `volumeAvailableCapacity` or any `NSURLVolume*` key — not in this
      workspace, not in `vendor/`, and not in any registry crate on either
      Apple target. No `sysinfo`, `fs2`, `fs4`, `dirs` or `directories` in
      either tree.

      The function that looks like it does is a red herring worth naming so it
      is not "rediscovered": `free_space` in
      `squallar-egui/src/ui_download_area.rs` is `quota?.free()`, arithmetic
      over an `OfflineQuota` whose two figures come from an HTTP GET to the
      service worker's `__quota__` route carrying
      `navigator.storage.estimate()`. A browser figure on the wasm target, no
      syscall, nothing on Apple. Stronger still: the field is never populated
      in production on any target — the only caller of `set_download_quota` is
      the test harness, and the state initialises it `None`.

  NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryActiveKeyboards
      Not used. `UITextInputMode::activeInputModes` exists in the tree only as
      an unused generated binding in `objc2-ui-kit`, with no caller in the
      workspace, `vendor/`, winit, or any crate in either Apple tree. An
      uncalled objc2 binding is dead-code-eliminated and its selector literal
      never reaches the binary's Objective-C metadata.

  NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryUserDefaults *is* declared above — read that
  entry rather than assuming this app stores preferences in it. It does not:
  persistence on iOS and macOS is the filesystem-backed `KvStore` in
  `squallar/src/kv.rs` (`create_dir_all` + `write` on a dedicated writer
  thread), and the web build uses `localStorage`, which is not this category
  either. The declaration is there for a dependency's theme probe.

  ── Tracking and collection ──────────────────────────────────────────────

  Both empty, and both are checked claims rather than the convenient answer.

  The app has no analytics, no advertising, no accounts and no telemetry. The
  one piece of personal data it handles at all is the device's location, and
  it never leaves the device: it draws a marker and a heading wedge on the
  map, and it chooses the nearest radar site from a static table compiled into
  the binary (`squallar_radar::sites::nearest_wsr88d_site`). The only request
  that carries coordinates is the Open-Meteo sounding fetch, and the
  coordinates it sends are the *radar site's*, read from that same static
  table — `squallar-app/src/app_fetch.rs`, `let (lat, lon) = (radar.lat,
  radar.lon)`. Apple's definition of "collect" is transmission off device, so
  there is nothing to declare.

  If an analytics SDK, a crash reporter, or any request carrying the user's
  own coordinates is ever added, NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes stops being
  empty and the App Store Connect privacy label has to change with it.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
    <false/>
    <key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
    <array/>
    <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
    <array/>
    <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
    <array>
        <dict>
            <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
            <string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategorySystemBootTime</string>
            <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
            <array>
                <string>35F9.1</string>
            </array>
        </dict>
        <dict>
            <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
            <string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryFileTimestamp</string>
            <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
            <array>
                <string>C617.1</string>
            </array>
        </dict>
        <dict>
            <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
            <string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryUserDefaults</string>
            <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
            <array>
                <string>CA92.1</string>
            </array>
        </dict>
    </array>
</dict>
</plist>
